1
00:00:00,072 --> 00:00:04,563
(Q11（单选）) I never really planned to be a lifeboat volunteer when I came to live in Northsea.

2
00:00:04,563 --> 00:00:12,215
[Q11干扰项（C）  website designer这份工作 interesting， 并不是 unsatisfying] I’d been  working in London as a website designer, but although that was interesting, I didn’t like city life.

3
00:00:12,215 --> 00:00:22,069
[Q11答案句（A） keen on = eager, pursue=develop, interest = hobby] I’d been really keen on boats as a teenager, and I thought if I went to live by the sea, I might be able to pursue that interest a bit more in my free time.

4
00:00:22,069 --> 00:00:29,136
Then I found that the Lifeboat Institution was looking for volunteers, so I decided to apply.

5
00:00:29,136 --> 00:00:36,807
(Q12) The Lifeboat Institution building here in Northsea’s hard to miss, it’s one of the largest in the country.&nbsp;

6
00:00:36,807 --> 00:00:43,824
[Q12答案句（B） the public = local resident] It was built 15 years ago with funds provided by a generous member of the public who’d lived here all her life.

7
00:00:43,824 --> 00:00:53,575
[Q12干扰项C： government=council, 排除：否定 rather than] As the Lifeboat Institution is a charity that relies on that kind of donation, rather than funding provided by the government, that was a huge help to us.

8
00:00:53,575 --> 00:01:01,288
(Q13) When I applied, I had to have a health assessment. The doctors were particularly interested in my vision.

9
00:01:01,288 --> 00:01:09,884
[Q13干扰项（B\C ） 排除依据：语义否定 已经做过手术， so ... was OK] I used to be short-sighted, so I’d had to wear glasses, but I’d had laser eye surgery two years earlier so that was OK.

10
00:01:09,884 --> 00:01:17,759
[Q13答案句（A）might have a problem = 题干限定词 concern] They gave me tests for colour blindness and they thought I might have a problem there, but it turned out I was OK.

11
00:01:17,759 --> 00:01:25,032
(Q14) When the coastguard gets an alert, all the volunteers are contacted and rush to the lifeboat station.

12
00:01:25,032 --> 00:01:34,597
[Q14答案句（B）+ Q14干扰项（A）  只是到station] Our target’s to get there in five minutes, then we try to get the boat off the dock and out to sea in another six to eight minutes.

13
00:01:34,597 --> 00:01:41,929
[Q14干扰项（C）] Our team’s proud that we usually achieve that – the average time across the country’s eight and a half minutes.

14
00:01:41,929 --> 00:01:50,648
(Q15) I’ve recently qualified as what’s called a ‘helmsman’, which means I have the ultimate responsibility for the lifeboat.

15
00:01:50,648 --> 00:02:00,115
[Q15干扰项B， equipment仅提及，检查是否正常运转。 不符合 decide] I have to check that the equipment we use is in working order – the crew have special life jackets that can support up to four people in the water.

16
00:02:00,115 --> 00:02:04,608
[Q15答案句（C） decision = decide， whether ... = if] And it’s ultimately my decision whether it’s safe to launch the boat.

17
00:02:04,608 --> 00:02:09,178
But it’s very rare not to launch it, even in the worst weather.

18
00:02:09,178 --> 00:02:14,512
(Q16) As well as going out on the lifeboat, my work involves other things too.

19
00:02:14,512 --> 00:02:29,628
[Q16答案句（A） speak to ... = give talks平行替换, problems = safety 解释型替换] A lot of people underestimate how quickly conditions can change at sea, so I speak to youth groups and sailing clubs in the area about the sorts of problems that sailors and swimmers can have if the weather suddenly gets bad.

20
00:02:29,628 --> 00:02:37,697
[Q16干扰项C&B： have volunteers表示已经有了，不需要recruit。    志愿者负责fundraise = raise money，不是David 负责] We also have a lot of volunteers who organise activities to raise money for us, and we couldn’t manage without them.

21
00:02:37,697 --> 00:02:51,491
(Q17&18（多选）) The training we get is a continuous process, focusing on technical competence and safe handling techniques, and it’s given me the confidence to deal with extreme situations without panicking.

22
00:02:51,491 --> 00:02:59,653
I was glad I’d done a first aid course before I started, as that’s a big help with the casualty care activities we do.

23
00:02:59,653 --> 00:03:06,259
We’ve done a lot on how to deal with ropes and tie knots –that’s an essential skill.

24
00:03:06,259 --> 00:03:12,239
After a year, I did a one-week residential course, led by specialists.

25
00:03:12,239 --> 00:03:23,370
They had a wave-tank where they could create extreme weather conditions – so we could get experience at what to do if the boat turned over in a storm at night, for example.

26
00:03:23,370 --> 00:03:32,240
(Q19&20) [团队支持与救援成就感] Since I started, I’ve had to deal with a range of emergency situations. But the work’s hugely motivating.

27
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:38,515
It’s not just about saving lives – I’ve learned a lot about the technology involved.

28
00:03:38,515 --> 00:03:45,328
My background in IT’s been useful here, and I can use my expertise to help other volunteers.

29
00:03:45,328 --> 00:03:53,857
They’re a great group – we’re like a family really, which helps when you’re dragging yourself out of bed on a cold stormy night.

30
00:03:53,857 --> 00:04:06,635
But actually, it’s the colder months that can be the most rewarding time. That’s when the incidents tend to be more serious, and you realise that you can make a huge difference to the outcome.

31
00:04:06,635 --> 00:04:09,135
So if any of you listeners are interested. . .

